<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#FFFFFF" />
    <base href="/" />
    @* No static <title> here: every page supplies its own through <PageTitle>, and with
       prerendering the HeadOutlet emits a real <title> element into this head. *@
    <meta name="description" content="bit Blow (Bit.Blow) - a Blazor-native library for node-based UIs, editors, flow charts and diagrams, a Blazor counterpart to React Flow, with interaction, layout and geometry written in C#." />

    @* Link previews: the card a chat client or a social site unfurls is the first impression a
       shared link makes. *@
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="Bit.Blow" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="Blow - node-based editors and diagrams for Blazor" />
    <meta property="og:description" content="The Blazor counterpart to React Flow: nodes, handles, edges, sub-flows, layout, undo and a minimap - with the interaction and the geometry written in C#." />
    <meta property="og:image" content="https://blow.bitplatform.dev/icon-512.png" />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" />

    <ResourcePreloader />
    @* The site kit (app.css, theme.js, site.js - shared by every Utilities demo), then this demo's
       own sample styles. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["demo.css"]" />
    @* The library's stylesheet. Unlike its script, it is not loaded on demand: without it the canvas,
       its nodes and its add-ons render unstyled. demo.css re-tints its --bit-blow-* properties from
       :root down, a step more specific than the .bit-blow they are declared on, so it wins from
       above this link as well. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["_content/Bit.Blow/bit-blow.css"]" />
    @* Scoped-CSS bundle: includes the client project's *.razor.css files as well. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["Bit.Blow.Demo.Server.styles.css"]" />
    @* Synchronous on purpose: applies the light/dark theme before first paint. *@
    <script src="@Assets["theme.js"]"></script>
    <ImportMap />
    <link rel="icon" href="favicon.ico" sizes="32x32" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="favicon.svg" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" sizes="512x512" href="icon-512.png" />
    <HeadOutlet @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />
</head>

@* data-enhance-nav="false": a link followed before the WebAssembly runtime has started is a plain
   page load rather than an enhanced one. Enhanced navigation fetches and merges the next page while
   the runtime is still starting, and the router the runtime then attaches can render the address
   it read before the merge - the URL says /getting-started and the page is the landing page. Once
   the app is interactive its router handles every link itself and this attribute is not consulted. *@
<body data-enhance-nav="false">
    @* InteractiveWebAssembly rather than Auto or Server: every pointer move of a drag, a pan or a
       connection is handled in C#, and on WebAssembly none of them waits on a network round trip.
       The pages are still prerendered, so the first response carries them. *@
    <Routes @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />
    <div id="blazor-error-ui" data-nosnippet>
        An unhandled error has occurred.
        <a href="." class="reload">Reload</a>
        <span class="dismiss">&#128473;</span>
    </div>
    <script src="@Assets["_framework/blazor.web.js"]"></script>
    <script src="@Assets["site.js"]"></script>
</body>

</html>
